# Line ending policy.
#
# Without this file the repository relied on core.autocrlf alone, which stored
# some CSVs with CRLF and others with LF. That made "git hash-object <file>"
# and "git hash-object --no-filters <file>" return different answers about
# whether a working tree file matched its committed blob, so a provenance audit
# could conclude that data had diverged when only the line endings differed.
# See simulation/diagnostics/defended_collapse_discrepancy_report.md, Anomaly A1.

# Documentation and tabular data are stored with LF in the repository.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.csv  text eol=lf
# JSON added 2026-09-08. Diagnostic manifests record SHA256 values computed on
# the working tree files. Without this, autocrlf stores LF and checks out CRLF on
# Windows, so a fresh clone would produce different bytes and every hash in a
# manifest would fail to verify. Same failure class as Anomaly A1 above.
*.json text eol=lf

# The attack vector revalidation evidence is a pinned, immutable record.
# Apply no line ending conversion to it in either direction, so that a raw byte
# comparison and a normalized hash comparison against the evidence tag always
# give the same answer. Seven of these blobs were committed with CRLF and must
# stay byte for byte as committed. Normalizing them would leave the normalized
# hash matching the tag while the raw byte comparison failed forever, which is
# the exact confusion this file exists to remove.
data/attack_vector_revalidation_v2/** -text

# Hash-manifested diagnostic artifacts. Their manifests record SHA256 values
# computed on the exact bytes the run produced on its Windows host, which are
# CRLF. Store them verbatim, overriding the LF rules above, so a fresh clone on
# any platform reproduces those bytes and the manifest verifies. Same rule and
# reason as the pinned evidence line above. Forward fix for future runs is to
# compute manifest hashes on LF-normalized bytes in the probe; these prefixes
# predate that and are pinned as produced.
simulation/diagnostics/drift_char_* -text

# Binary assets, never converted.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.mp4 binary
